WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.095 --> 00:00:08.470
MELIKA GOLKARAM:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.950
I hope you've had a
great Google Next so far.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.450
And thank you so much for
attending this session.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:14.780
My name is Melika Golkaram.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.090
I'm a media specialist customer
engineer from Google Cloud.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.140
And today, I have the pleasure
to co-present this session

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.850
with two industry experts,
Jeff Webb, a principal solution

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:32.310
design architect from
Sky, and Moore Macauley.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.005
He's a director of
product from Harmonic.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:43.180
So what are we hearing
in this session?

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.000
Well, I think it's
useful for all of us

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.920
to go a little bit
back and look at what's

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.700
happened in the industry, how
things have shifted and changed

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:55.800
until we've got to this
point, and go to 2018 and 2019

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.850
and see what Google Cloud
has done for media customers

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.890
to help them achieve
their targets

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.280
and implement their
interesting projects.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.070
And then I'll ask Jeff
to come up the stage

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.590
and take us through
their interesting journey

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.940
of designing and deploying a
future-proof sports streaming

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.830
platform.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.920
And then, Moore is
going to give us

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.730
a different angle of how
they implement it there,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:26.640
basically stream processing
applications on top

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:32.960
of containers to help Sky
in this wonderful project.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.950
So this is really
interesting for all of us.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.670
Today, we are all so used
to get any sort of content

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.840
with great quality on
any device in a way

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.080
that we all forget that this
industry is actually not

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.670
that old.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.550
If you look at 2005
when, for the first time,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.950
YouTube was launched
as, basically,

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.340
the biggest streaming
platform in the world.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.980
10 years down the line,
things shifted and changed

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:02.340
in every different way.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.570
Streaming protocols, transcoding
formats, infrastructure grew.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.090
Big data-- everything
changed so rapidly

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.480
that 10 years down
the line, we see

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.070
a 4% fall from the
traditional TV viewership.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:23.770
And that figure lends itself
into a 74% increase in content

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.190
viewership on YouTube.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:30.490
So this figure looks a little
bit odd, because 4% versus 74%.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:32.800
Part of it is because
the overall viewership

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.240
of content on traditional
TV was a huge number.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.600
So when you lend
it into YouTube,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.060
it becomes a really big factor.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.520
But the other part was
that people overall

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.370
started to watch content a
lot more than they used to

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:48.310
before, because obviously, you
could find any sort of niche

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.960
content you wouldn't be able
to find on TV on YouTube.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.240
Three years later--
this is interesting--

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.180
the overall viewership
of content on streaming

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.170
platforms--

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:01.150
not only YouTube,
but everywhere--

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.360
witnessed a 35%
increase year on year.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.240
And then today,
where we're standing,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.930
people are consuming one
billion hours of content

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:11.860
only on YouTube.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:14.450
That's a massive number.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.370
So that makes us hopeful
that for the next five years,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.570
the profit that gets shifted
away from traditional TV

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.150
platforms, IPTV, cable TV--

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.420
and this $30 billion
profit will be

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:31.810
spent on online TV platforms.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.690
So that will take
us to 2025, where

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:38.380
half of the viewers
of the age of below 32

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.650
will never actually see a
subscription on traditional TV

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.640
platforms.

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.070
And that will take
the consumption

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:50.110
of media on internet to
25% of the world data.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.860
So we know that things
have changed so much.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.080
We're really proud to be
standing in this moment.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.870
But we want to see
if Cloud is going

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:05.670
to be the next big
wave of disruption

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.380
into the media and tech
industry, at the same time.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.690
So let's see why Cloud
matters in this industry.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:18.250
So problem number one of
every technology company

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:19.910
is the speed to delivery.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:23.320
If you're a media company,
your problem is a lot bigger.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.340
Why?

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.940
You're acquiring a lot
of content from studios,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.460
from sports organizations--
this massive amount of money

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.660
you're spending.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.150
And you want to make sure that
you're launching the service

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.630
right on time, you
are actually providing

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.520
any sort of necessary
infrastructure

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.715
without having to worry about
infrastructure planning.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.580
And the other problem
about media industry

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.610
is that it's not only
about applications.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:52.292
It's also about the delivery
of media across the world.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:54.250
So you need to make sure
that your content gets

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.530
encoded, transcoded,
is being sent

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:58.240
across the world
with minimum delay.

00:05:00.583 --> 00:05:02.000
The other problem
is that when you

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.743
work in a really
fast environment,

00:05:04.743 --> 00:05:06.410
you need to be sure
that you're scaling,

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:08.120
but you're not
constantly calculating

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:11.870
and you're not being stingy
with your infrastructure,

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.920
because you want to make
sure your users are not

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.090
getting delays, or that you
wouldn't lose your viewers

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.350
to your competition.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.150
A very good example of
that is news content.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.070
So if you lose one or two
minutes of content viewership,

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.383
on a kind of delivery
on your network,

00:05:30.383 --> 00:05:32.300
then people would just
go to your competition.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:35.720
And then you're using millions
of dollars of ads for something

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.970
pretty plain and simple.

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.190
So with Cloud, you can
actually do this scalability

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:40.910
pretty fast.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.340
And then you just shut
down your instances.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.070
And after that,
you didn't really

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.410
have to worry about a
bunch of annoying servers

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:51.560
in your infrastructure.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.930
Then again, you can have any
sort of analytics you want.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.180
You can think about what you
want to do with it later.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.640
You can be super
generous with your data.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.880
And you can also
use that data later,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.120
once you figure out what
data set is important for you

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.270
to have intelligent
decision-making with machine

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:07.770
learning.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:10.700
And machine learning is
infrastructure demanding.

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.070
So with Cloud, you can
actually fix that problem.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.530
And then the sweet story
of studio requirements.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:19.470
So if you're a
content distributor

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.890
and you work with multiple
different studios, each of them

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.320
comes to you with
a massive contract

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.700
of different requirements
for your infrastructure.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.320
And if you work with
a cloud provider,

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.400
you really don't have to worry
about at least the basics

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.920
of these requirements.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.370
With all said and
done, we still think

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.740
hybrid solutions
are going to stay

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.740
with media industry for a
while from now, because you

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.020
have your transcoders,
encoders on premise.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.110
And you want to
make sure that you

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:50.510
are keeping this infrastructure,
but burst into cloud

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:51.870
whenever it's needed.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:52.940
And here we are.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.330
We are in Google
Cloud infrastructure.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.130
When you look at
that map, you really

00:06:58.130 --> 00:07:00.590
shouldn't see a lot of
colorful dots and fibers--

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.620
even though it's entertaining
to look at it that way, too.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.960
But you should
actually look at this

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.200
as a representation
of six Google services

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:12.950
that have more than one
billion subscribers.

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:16.100
All these services
are sending traffic

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:19.430
securely with a
lot of scalability

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:21.890
through this network, through
these points of presence.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:25.790
And Cloud is using very
much the same infrastructure

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:30.290
that YouTube, Gmail, Maps,
and those services are using.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.230
Over the past three years,
we have spent $20 billion

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:36.890
on infrastructure to maintain
and expand our network.

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.340
We have invested in
13 sub-sea cables.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:46.370
We've launched in 18 different
regions, 55 zones, 134 network

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:51.810
edges, and 90 CDN locations,
plus 73 interconnect locations.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:54.960
We recently launched
in Switzerland.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.340
And we are launching in
Indonesia and in Japan

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:01.410
for the second time.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:05.630
But we know success
doesn't come from wealth.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.878
What made Google
Google is technology.

00:08:07.878 --> 00:08:09.920
So if you look at different
layers of technology,

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.040
here, you see we
build our own chips.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:14.870
We build our own hardware.

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.220
We build our own data centers.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:18.530
We maintain it.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:21.380
We invented Kubernetes.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.280
All of our services,
across the board,

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:28.010
use Kubernetes is to
scale fast and easily.

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:29.750
We have multiple
layers of identity

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.330
to control access
to our platform.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:35.360
If you look at how much content
is being stored and uploaded

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:41.299
in YouTube, you will see
storage is not an issue

00:08:41.299 --> 00:08:45.710
that we would worry about-- and
the security around it, either.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:47.930
We talked about our
backbone, but it's sitting

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:49.730
on top of everything else.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:53.030
And the operational
and security of devices

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:59.120
is something that our SREs are
paying attention to every day.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.690
So how would your service
look like, ultimately?

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.530
If you're a kind of
visual studio company

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:10.333
and you have people
sitting in Los Angeles,

00:09:10.333 --> 00:09:12.000
in London, different
parts of the world,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.800
you have content stored
in multiple locations,

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.260
you want to make
sure that you can

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:19.098
do your editing on
the device that is not

00:09:19.098 --> 00:09:21.390
present in your data center,
but gives you the illusion

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.850
of having it at your vicinity.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:27.600
If you are a live sports
company like Jeff is,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.390
you basically want to make
sure that your content is

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.780
being distributed around
the world without any delay.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:38.490
If you are a telco and
you've acquired other telcos

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.990
and you are distributing
to multiple locations,

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:43.980
you want to make sure that
your studio rights are

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:50.070
being obeyed, basically.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:54.048
And finally, when you look
at the amount of storage

00:09:54.048 --> 00:09:55.590
you will be needing,
you really don't

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:57.330
want to worry about
how much content

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:58.710
is being stored every day.

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:00.300
So with Google Cloud,
we're giving you

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:04.770
a huge amount of infrastructure
to scale your services.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:08.520
We give you containers,
Kubernetes app deployment

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.670
services where you can deploy
with a lot of scalability

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.710
and reach your goals.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:18.600
We give you an 11-9
durable storage--

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:23.030
different classes, but instant
access across the board

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:24.930
to store your content.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:28.320
Your network is basically a
global network that is well

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.030
connected with our fiber.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.000
And your services
are going to be

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.400
distributed in different
virtual private networks.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:39.040
But because they're within
Google infrastructure,

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.670
the security is
complied and the content

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:45.580
is being encrypted at
rest and at transit.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.730
We also give you a lot
of big data and machine

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.460
learning capabilities to
sit on top of your services,

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.700
to give you intelligent
ideas about how you can push

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:58.650
for better business
decisions and for maintaining

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:01.770
your customers on your platform.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.890
This picture always
cracks me up.

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:08.160
In 2010, this
picture went online.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:11.460
And it drove the whole
internet crazy about whether it

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:12.990
was Tom Hanks or Bill Murray.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.940
What do you think?

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:19.500
Thank you.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.750
Yes, it's Bill Murray.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:25.080
The whole point is,
with the whole hype

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:27.330
about cloud these
days, there is so much

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.250
similarity between
cloud platforms

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.680
that people sometimes
get confused

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.060
about what would be the best
decision for their own business

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:36.810
idea.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.570
And we think there
are certain things

00:11:39.570 --> 00:11:42.930
in Google Cloud that shines
among all other services.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:45.900
We are investing a lot
in hybrid approaches.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.860
Cloud storage is a
very special service,

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.510
especially when
it comes to media.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.240
We give you a metadata
caching, redundancy,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.010
duplication across the board.

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.120
It's multi-regional.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.550
And regardless of
what class of storage

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:04.460
you put your content on, it's
always instant accessibility.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:06.690
Our CDN is very
easy to configure.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:09.720
So it's literally just
putting a tick at a box,

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:12.540
and then content gets
cached everywhere.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:15.310
We have a new approach
to machine learning.

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.983
We use Auto ML, which gives
you access to our models.

00:12:18.983 --> 00:12:20.650
But you can train it
with your own data.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:24.150
So you can get rid of a
lot of unnecessary labels

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.840
that you would get
from a generic model.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.030
And the network that we
talked about several times--

00:12:31.030 --> 00:12:34.550
it helps you distribute
your content a lot faster.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.860
2018-- it was
interesting for us.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:41.850
We launched in eight
new regions and we

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.880
took Dedicated and
Partner Interconnect

00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:47.550
to general availability.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.820
With rendering, we rewrote
the whole Zync platform.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.420
And it's available on
Google infrastructure,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:58.920
and provides up to
48,000 cores to editors,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.350
and individual editors.

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:06.000
We released a live
machine learning service

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.240
for video analysis in real time.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.360
We released a lot of features
for metadata enrichment,

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.140
and also for monetization, like
video Auto ML, object tracking,

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:18.420
and stuff like that.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.480
In terms of OTT, AVATA, which
is our end-to-end platform,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:22.950
launched in Europe.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:26.370
And our CVN, in terms
of media, went on GA.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:31.250
And Cloud armor was launched
to protect your service.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.130
And we are doing a lot
of interesting stuff

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.840
with archiving.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:38.800
So tape digitization as a
part of bigger Google services

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:40.900
and art and culture
helps our customers

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:44.320
that have valuable content to
digitize those tapes with very

00:13:44.320 --> 00:13:47.620
little money and then use
machine learning to enrich

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:48.970
the metadata for those assets.

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:54.960
And what's coming in 2019?

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:59.030
We are launching five new
sea cables across the board.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:02.720
Hybrid platforms are going
to stay as our highest

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.100
priority this year.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:06.710
So that will help you
work with your streaming

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:09.890
services, your different
types of-- if you have machine

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:12.950
learning stuff on premise,
and a lot of different aspects

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.160
of hybrid workflows
or in different cloud

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:20.960
providers with one control plane
and the same set of APIs that

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.380
are basically industry standards
like Kubernetes, for example.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:30.380
We are replacing
our MPAA licensing

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:35.090
with TPN, which covers a lot
more broadcasters and content

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:36.410
owners.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:41.460
That will be available on
major GCP infrastructure.

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.880
And we are doing a lot
of CDN enhancements.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.360
We are working on
providing media

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:50.870
APIs for encoding,
transcoding, and ad insertion.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:53.240
We are releasing a
neutral rendering

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:55.310
platform called OpenCue.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:59.630
That's independent of any
specific software, like Zync.

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:04.010
And we are working
on other AI services

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:06.830
to go to general availability.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.920
And something very interesting
is about edge TPUs--

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.680
that you will be able to
download the model on a device

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:15.980
offline and the device
will do the analysis

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:18.860
and send the results
to the cloud.

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.880
So with that, I'm going to ask
Jeff to come up to the stage

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.610
and take us through their
interesting story, which

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.950
is very inspirational.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.910
JEFF WEBB: I'm going to talk
over the next 20 minutes

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:33.330
about live sports in the cloud.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:35.670
We've been doing it for
a long time on premise.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.370
And there's lots of
opportunities in the cloud.

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.083
So my name's Jeff Webb.

00:15:40.083 --> 00:15:41.500
I'm principal
streaming architect.

00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:44.220
I'm from Sky UK,
hence the accent.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.520
So what I'm going to
talk about is about Sky.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:52.320
So for those not familiar with
who we are, go through that.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:57.340
Believe in better streaming,
which is part of our ethos.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:00.560
Success formula for delivering
live streaming service.

00:16:00.560 --> 00:16:02.175
So what does that make?

00:16:02.175 --> 00:16:03.550
Software Defined
Streaming-- this

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:06.730
is our concept for the
next generation platform.

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:11.118
How Cloud benefits, and
comparison of on-premise.

00:16:11.118 --> 00:16:12.910
We have a thing called
single channel fault

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:15.640
domain, which is really kind of
where the key innovation comes

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:16.300
in.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:17.980
And I'm going to
show you what happens

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:20.540
when something goes wrong.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:24.700
So about Sky-- we have 23.6
million customers in Europe.

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:27.190
And we operate in seven
European countries.

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:29.780
We're very proud, now, to be
part of the Comcast family.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.165
So that acquisition went
through a few months ago.

00:16:32.165 --> 00:16:34.900
Sky Q, which is our
setup box proposition

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.330
in Europe-- and that's
in 5.5 million homes.

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:40.930
That's up from 3.4
million last year.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.300
We're increasing investment
in original drama,

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:45.830
for a 25% increase
over last year.

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.903
And very interestingly, if
you produce really good drama,

00:16:48.903 --> 00:16:50.320
then more customers
will watch it.

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:53.220
So you can see viewing has more
than doubled in the last three

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:54.940
years.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:57.667
Put this up here because this
is something else, as well.

00:16:57.667 --> 00:17:00.250
I'm really proud to be part of
a socially responsible company.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:02.922
So we have things
like Sky Ocean Rescue.

00:17:02.922 --> 00:17:05.380
And you might have seen things
across the internet-- things

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:07.480
about trying to save the oceans.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:10.542
There's a lot of plastic that
we throw away and ends up

00:17:10.542 --> 00:17:12.250
in the animals and
the whales and things.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:15.079
And it's not a good thing.

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.494
So believe in better streaming.

00:17:17.494 --> 00:17:19.869
Going to take you through a
little journey of the last 10

00:17:19.869 --> 00:17:20.680
years.

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:23.050
I myself have been
at Sky for 14 years.

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.810
I've been in the
streaming team since 2009.

00:17:25.810 --> 00:17:27.579
So over that 10-year
period, we've

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:29.590
gone through three
different generations.

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:31.090
We started off, as
most broadcasters

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:35.650
did, in 2009 when Apple
announced the HLS spec.

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:37.420
We wanted to be able
to do live sports--

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:39.890
Premier League and things.

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:41.960
And so we did what
most broadcasters did

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:45.730
They went out to market,
went and did a comparison, go

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:49.162
through the RFI, RFP process,
bought some white boxes,

00:17:49.162 --> 00:17:50.620
plugged them in in
the data center.

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:52.160
You had video in on one side.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:53.770
You had video out
on the other side.

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.690
And customers were happy.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:59.260
Fast forward several
years, business changes.

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.090
Customers want higher quality.

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.760
You've gone from standard
definition phones.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:05.900
Now you've got retina
displays on your phones

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:07.480
and your tablets.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.790
And so customers are expecting
to get a better quality.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:13.040
Obviously, you've also got big
screens, smart TVs, and so on.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:15.460
So we went to generation 2.

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:19.300
Problem with the first model is
that you go and you try and buy

00:18:19.300 --> 00:18:22.470
the biggest box you can afford
because you want to maximize

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:23.600
your channel density.

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.640
So you put five channels
on this big box.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:28.600
And this box cost
you like $20,000.

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.660
If you want to get more,
then you spend more.

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:33.547
That business model
is fine up until you

00:18:33.547 --> 00:18:34.630
have to go and replace it.

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:37.197
So every few years, about
every three years or so,

00:18:37.197 --> 00:18:39.280
you have to go through the
upgrade, refresh cycle.

00:18:39.280 --> 00:18:41.170
And that's a very
expensive way of doing it.

00:18:41.170 --> 00:18:44.430
In reality, as broadcaster,
all you need is the software.

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.180
The software is the
intellectual property.

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:49.560
So that's what we
did in generation 2.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:52.210
We said, why would
I buy your server?

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:53.590
I just want your software.

00:18:53.590 --> 00:18:56.020
We can run our own server,
as most enterprises do.

00:18:56.020 --> 00:18:57.460
We run VMware.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:58.510
So that's what we did.

00:18:58.510 --> 00:18:59.653
We virtualized it.

00:18:59.653 --> 00:19:01.570
And we've run that
platform for six years now.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:03.640
And that's been very successful.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:06.790
What I'm talking today about
is moving to the cloud,

00:19:06.790 --> 00:19:08.530
specifically for
sports, because there's

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:11.980
a really great
opportunity for that.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:13.820
So just to give
you some context,

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:17.170
what is the success formula
for delivering a live streaming

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:17.670
service?

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:18.462
This can be sports.

00:19:18.462 --> 00:19:19.300
It can be anything.

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:24.110
Well, firstly, it
starts with content.

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:27.380
Leaving aside the technology,
customers in the pay TV

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.130
operator-- they want
to be entertained.

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:30.380
They want to watch the sports.

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:32.720
They want to watch the
"Game of Thrones" next week.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.300
They want to watch Italian or
German football, and so on.

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:36.470
That is what they pay for.

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:37.800
That's part of the experience.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.180
And so we shouldn't forget that.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:43.050
At Sky, I'm part of what's
called software engineering.

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:46.190
Software engineering is
actually quite a lot of people.

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:48.650
In the building I'm
in, there's 800 or so

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:50.510
that are in software
engineering.

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:52.655
And these guys and
girls are very clever.

00:19:52.655 --> 00:19:54.530
And they basically go
and write the software.

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:57.560
Now, unlike a lot
of other people,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:19:59.070
we give the software
away for free.

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:02.840
So the network is
obviously key to this.

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:05.245
So if you own and
operate your own network,

00:20:05.245 --> 00:20:07.370
then, obviously, you can
ensure that that last mile

00:20:07.370 --> 00:20:10.100
connection, especially,
is, obviously, optimized.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:11.990
In the UK, we offer
Sky Broadband,

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:16.205
which is our second-largest
broadband provider in the UK.

00:20:16.205 --> 00:20:17.010
And this is now--

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.280
I think it's about
six million customers.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:21.320
Sky Broadband is also
rolling out in Sky Italy

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:22.610
later this year.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.400
Obviously, have lots
of CDNs, because we

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:28.580
have lots of traffic
and lots of customers--

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:30.770
which segues nicely
onto customers.

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:34.040
So here's what a Sky
Q box looks like.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:37.400
We have, now, TVs that use Roku
and, obviously, mobile devices,

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.410
smart TVs, consoles, and so on.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:42.710
Ultimately, if you take
content plus developers

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:48.980
plus network plus customers,
it all gives you quality.

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:51.852
Because lots of customers,
they'll want to do the thing,

00:20:51.852 --> 00:20:53.310
watch the content
at the same time.

00:20:53.310 --> 00:20:55.050
So next Monday is
a good example.

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:57.070
You'll have "Game of
Thrones" on there at 9 PM.

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:00.617
It's going to be simulcast
between US and Europe.

00:21:00.617 --> 00:21:02.450
Customers want to watch
it on their devices.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.283
They want to watch it
together, because it's

00:21:04.283 --> 00:21:06.350
a social experience, just
like when you get Super

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:09.760
Bowl or Olympics and things.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:13.880
And they want to watch it in HD
because that's what they want.

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.520
So software-defined streaming.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.510
This is reimagining of
how we deliver and go.

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:22.940
We came up with this
interesting concept

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:25.152
called Streaming DevOps,
because we've been

00:21:25.152 --> 00:21:26.360
doing streaming for 10 years.

00:21:26.360 --> 00:21:28.190
And we've been
doing it very well,

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:30.390
and obviously, customers
are happy with that.

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:32.160
But we wanted to make it better.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:35.350
And so it's been part
of software engineering.

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:37.460
We looked out and
asked our colleagues

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:40.520
and said, how can we take
what other teams within Sky

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:43.640
are doing from the DevOps world
and bring that into streaming?

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:44.797
So we created something.

00:21:44.797 --> 00:21:45.380
We made it up.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:46.775
It's called Streaming DevOps.

00:21:46.775 --> 00:21:48.770
And really, it's four
things that come into it.

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:50.690
First is RAM resilience.

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:53.980
So as a broadcaster,
you're giving the customer

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:55.490
the experience.

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:58.700
In live sports, you can't
afford to have downtime

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:00.732
because if they've
missed the goal,

00:22:00.732 --> 00:22:01.940
it's not going to come again.

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:04.550
They're going to have to watch
on the repeat or something.

00:22:04.550 --> 00:22:06.680
So you want to try and
avoid that at all costs.

00:22:06.680 --> 00:22:09.618
We came up with the Single
Channel Fault Domain concept.

00:22:09.618 --> 00:22:11.660
I'll show you what that
is in a couple of slides.

00:22:11.660 --> 00:22:13.100
And also, I'll talk,
I'll give you the example

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.280
of how it's self-healing.

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.610
So basically, if
you have a problem,

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:17.840
don't show the customer.

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:20.927
Hide the problem until
you've resolved it.

00:22:20.927 --> 00:22:22.010
Channels must stay on air.

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:23.570
And that's critical.

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:25.310
Geographic resilience
is very key

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:28.623
because a simple
analogy would be,

00:22:28.623 --> 00:22:31.040
if you've got one data center,
don't put all your channels

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:33.050
in one data center
because chances are,

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:35.035
one day, something will
go wrong and you'll

00:22:35.035 --> 00:22:37.410
lose not just one channel,
but a whole bunch of channels.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.140
So have two baskets.

00:22:39.140 --> 00:22:40.430
Do exactly the same.

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:43.100
Make sure the baskets are
identical with your channels.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:45.500
And you'll be a lot better.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:47.780
In order to do that, you
need highly available

00:22:47.780 --> 00:22:49.790
synchronized video streaming.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:52.040
And that's what we'll
show a little bit later.

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:54.370
The second pillar is
around speed to market.

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.836
Melika touched on it earlier.

00:22:56.836 --> 00:22:58.970
The problem with
enterprise is that you

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:01.210
have to go through
the procurement cycle.

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:04.020
If we order a server today,
six to eight weeks later,

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:06.140
the server will arrive,
it'll get plugged in.

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:07.670
It's too long.

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:10.970
How many football matches,
how many basketballs games

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:12.060
have you missed by then?

00:23:12.060 --> 00:23:15.390
Well, quite a lot, especially
if it's March Madness.

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:18.428
Automatic pipeline-- so taking
some of the benefits of what's

00:23:18.428 --> 00:23:19.220
going on in DevOps.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:21.950
So the continuous integration,
continuous deployment.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:25.130
I'm sure, obviously, a lot of
you Googlers and other people--

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:27.716
don't need to
explain what that is.

00:23:27.716 --> 00:23:31.440
The third pillar
is observability.

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:32.600
We're dealing with video.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:33.810
We're a TV company.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:37.282
So when that "Game of Thrones"
episode is coming through,

00:23:37.282 --> 00:23:39.740
you want to make sure that,
before it gets to the customer,

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:42.340
that it's actually good video
and good audio coming out.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:44.000
Because if you had
a lip sync issue,

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:45.635
it's a bad customer experience.

00:23:45.635 --> 00:23:47.510
So you want to make sure
that your monitoring

00:23:47.510 --> 00:23:50.030
systems before you actually
get it to the customer

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:50.840
is actually good.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:54.943
And doing that in an automated
way is the way to go.

00:23:54.943 --> 00:23:56.360
Obviously, for
operations reasons,

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:57.985
you want to be able
to have centralized

00:23:57.985 --> 00:24:00.830
logging and monitoring using
open-source tools like the ELK

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:03.830
Stack and also
real-time dashboards--

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:06.020
things like using Grafana
and Prometheus, as well.

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:08.240
So you can give
ops guys actually

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.880
what's going on right
now with customers

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.400
and see how the
platform is performing.

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:15.650
And the fourth one, really,
is actually the key one.

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:17.750
And the reason
why I'm here today

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:19.910
is talking about
transportability.

00:24:19.910 --> 00:24:23.420
At the outset, we had
a desire, a principle

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.590
to make the platform agnostic.

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:28.440
So on premise, we run in VMware.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:33.580
So now, we're taking it
into Google with GCP.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:36.588
Just to mention there about GKE,
we're not doing GKE on premise,

00:24:36.588 --> 00:24:38.380
but I just heard the
announcement yesterday

00:24:38.380 --> 00:24:39.410
that it's coming.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:47.798
So how does the cloud benefit
live sports streaming?

00:24:47.798 --> 00:24:48.590
Fairly obvious one.

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:50.660
It shortens the
development cycle

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:52.880
because you're not having
to wait for infrastructure.

00:24:52.880 --> 00:24:55.940
We can spin up, now,
channels in minutes.

00:24:55.940 --> 00:24:58.260
So everything's pre-configured.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:01.078
So if you take a
Formula One example,

00:25:01.078 --> 00:25:03.620
those Grand Prixs are run, on
average, about every two weeks.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:06.500
Sometimes every other week,
dependant on the geography.

00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:08.430
But sometimes,
it's a bit longer.

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.190
But on Friday, they
have their practice.

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:12.440
Saturday, you have qualifying.

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:15.020
And Sunday, you have the race.

00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:17.310
Each of those channels
must be consistently built.

00:25:17.310 --> 00:25:19.670
So if I have the
Australian Grand Prix

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:22.400
and then, two weeks later, I
have the Singapore Grand Prix,

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:24.910
and then a week later, I have
the Bahrain Grand Prix, then

00:25:24.910 --> 00:25:28.150
the actual channel configuration
itself is the same.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:30.650
The only thing that's really
changed over those three events

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:33.770
is the time and, actually,
the content itself.

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:35.452
The way that I
deliver it to a client

00:25:35.452 --> 00:25:36.660
is still the same end points.

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:40.900
When a customer presses
Play, it's still the same.

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:43.270
Each channel in
the configuration

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.010
must be built consistently.

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:47.822
We have a a YAML file,
another markup language.

00:25:47.822 --> 00:25:50.030
It has all the configuration
parameters for channels.

00:25:50.030 --> 00:25:52.120
So you would have things
like your A/B ladder.

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:53.220
You would have DRM types.

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:56.298
You'd have stream
formats and so on.

00:25:56.298 --> 00:25:57.840
This is really
critical, because this

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:01.170
allows you to maintain
or become stateless.

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:04.110
So your channel configuration
can be stateless.

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:07.120
This is very necessary
for reasons of continuity

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:08.610
at the moment.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:10.440
We wanted a platform
to be modular.

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:13.740
So, obviously, technology
changes from time to time.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:18.250
Some smart people will go and
invent new codecs and things

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:20.790
for new devices and stuff.

00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:23.560
Things will come along
and things will go away.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:25.590
So you need it to be
modular so that you're not

00:26:25.590 --> 00:26:27.630
locked into a
particular technology

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:31.920
when something much better can
come along and possibly even be

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:34.270
cheaper, as well.

00:26:34.270 --> 00:26:35.360
Cost effective.

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:39.610
So, obviously, make sure that
you can do it cost effectively.

00:26:39.610 --> 00:26:41.880
Speed to market--
fairly obvious.

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.650
End-to-end automation pipeline.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:46.270
Do everything automatically.

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:49.810
Do not have engineers going
into graphical user interfaces,

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.940
manually putting
in configurations,

00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:54.610
and pressing Apply.

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:56.350
We used to do that.

00:26:56.350 --> 00:26:58.620
Not doing that anymore.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:00.680
So increase reliability
and protection--

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:01.930
a Single Channel Fault Domain.

00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:02.870
I'll show you that.

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:05.000
And a really interesting
thing that we're now

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.920
able to do in the cloud, because
it's much more cost effective,

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.130
is around A/B
multivariate testing.

00:27:10.130 --> 00:27:13.240
So imagine being able to
have a Sky Sports channel.

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:14.990
Let's say it's Formula
One, because that's

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:16.410
on the next slide.

00:27:16.410 --> 00:27:18.140
And that's the standard version.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.520
Then what we have
is a B version of it

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:21.900
which is slightly different.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:24.150
So you make the B version
available to some customers.

00:27:24.150 --> 00:27:26.300
You try what it is
in the real world.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.740
And you get feedback on
it, like you would do

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:30.830
going through a trials process.

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:33.100
This is video, so you
actually need to see it.

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:35.210
You need the data.

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:37.080
So it's quite key.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.980
This new platform allows us
to maintain channel uptime.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:41.720
So that's really critical.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:45.670
Software upgrades is
a classic problem.

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:50.670
So in this diagram, I've
taken a layered approach.

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:52.710
My arm's not that big.

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:54.860
So other top, you've
got Sky clients.

00:27:54.860 --> 00:27:57.010
You've got Sky Go,
Now TV, Sky Sports,

00:27:57.010 --> 00:28:01.887
Sky Q. On the next layer, we've
got content delivery networks.

00:28:01.887 --> 00:28:03.220
Obviously, there's many of them.

00:28:03.220 --> 00:28:06.000
We've got video
workflow with Harmonic.

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:07.510
We've got core services layer.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:09.245
So this is a sort of wrapper.

00:28:09.245 --> 00:28:10.620
We'll see that on
the next slide.

00:28:10.620 --> 00:28:11.453
We're using Jenkins.

00:28:11.453 --> 00:28:14.430
The sort of ketchup bottle
is what we call Sauce.

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.770
Sauce is our configuration
management tool

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.362
that we've written in-house.

00:28:18.362 --> 00:28:19.320
We're using Prometheus.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:20.550
We've got Grafana.

00:28:20.550 --> 00:28:22.500
We've got Puppet
for orchestration.

00:28:22.500 --> 00:28:26.610
We've got ELK Stack for
logging and information.

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.410
We've got container
and orchestration

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:29.958
with Docker Engine
and Kubernetes.

00:28:29.958 --> 00:28:31.500
We do run our own
Kubernetes cluster.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.800
We're not using
GKE at the moment.

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.430
And, really, what
I would call out

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:37.930
as the only difference
is the bottom layer.

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:39.710
So you've got on-premise
infrastructure,

00:28:39.710 --> 00:28:43.530
your typical Intel Xeon
processors, et cetera,

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:46.710
that cost you a lot of money and
generally go out to date pretty

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:49.620
quickly because the upgrade
cycle on servers is pretty

00:28:49.620 --> 00:28:50.520
fast--

00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:52.383
12 to 18 months.

00:28:52.383 --> 00:28:54.550
And then on the bottom layer
on the right-hand side,

00:28:54.550 --> 00:28:56.920
you've got a bunch
of cloud stuff--

00:28:56.920 --> 00:28:58.000
hence we being here.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.190
So we'll go through.

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:01.500
We've got Google Compute
Engine, which we're using.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.290
We've got Google Cloud
Storage, Google Load Balancing,

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:07.230
Cloud Router because
we're dealing with video.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:09.990
We need to reliably get
the video to the cloud,

00:29:09.990 --> 00:29:11.340
not trusting the internet.

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:13.403
Could use the internet.

00:29:13.403 --> 00:29:14.070
That's possible.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:17.100
But you want to take a more
reliable approach to it.

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:20.498
So using Google Direct Connect,
having redundant 10-gig links

00:29:20.498 --> 00:29:22.290
delivering multiple
channels into the cloud

00:29:22.290 --> 00:29:24.720
is the sensible way to go.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:28.170
You need firewalls there because
you need to protect your data.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:31.740
And you've got NAT and the
entire access management--

00:29:31.740 --> 00:29:32.625
to name but a few.

00:29:32.625 --> 00:29:34.500
And really, that's the
only difference there,

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.640
is that bottom infrastructure.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:41.270
So what does single-channel
fault domain look like?

00:29:41.270 --> 00:29:46.070
So I've got an example here
with Formula One channel.

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:50.260
So I appreciates it's a
little bit busy diagram.

00:29:50.260 --> 00:29:55.660
So working through, from the
top, in the blue box of the top

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:57.550
there, we've got SDS, top layer.

00:29:57.550 --> 00:30:00.887
It managers live
channel configuration.

00:30:00.887 --> 00:30:02.470
That's the YAML files
and stuff that I

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:04.050
was talking about before--

00:30:04.050 --> 00:30:06.637
your 5 meg, your 4 meg, your
3 meg, your 2 meg, your 1 meg,

00:30:06.637 --> 00:30:07.220
your half meg.

00:30:07.220 --> 00:30:09.965
When you A/B a ladder for
customers, your protocols,

00:30:09.965 --> 00:30:11.590
your edge list, your
Smooth, your DASH.

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:14.990
DRM types and stuff because
you're a paid TV operator.

00:30:14.990 --> 00:30:17.890
And it also manages
capacity, as well.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:21.520
So we were in our own
Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:23.070
And so we managed
the capacity on it

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:24.820
to make sure that we
have enough resources

00:30:24.820 --> 00:30:27.100
to be able to deliver the
video at the right time,

00:30:27.100 --> 00:30:29.940
in the right place.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.040
It also manages the
metrics, as well.

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:33.270
So it's pulling all that in.

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:35.353
And what it's doing-- it's
aggregating the metrics

00:30:35.353 --> 00:30:36.590
from the whole stack.

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:38.120
So this is
everything, basically,

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:40.640
coming in from the left-hand
side to the right-hand side.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:43.305
It's also pulling in the
client-side metrics, as well.

00:30:43.305 --> 00:30:44.930
So you actually get
an end-to-end view,

00:30:44.930 --> 00:30:47.660
which is really, really
cool for operations people

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:49.412
because generally,
they often complain

00:30:49.412 --> 00:30:50.870
they don't have
enough information,

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:52.578
or they don't have
the right information.

00:30:52.578 --> 00:30:54.920
So trying to fix that and
give them that visibility

00:30:54.920 --> 00:30:56.970
is really key.

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:58.860
The purple box on
the left hand side--

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.610
it says, SDS video and
quality monitoring.

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:06.410
So its purpose is to monitor
the output of the mezzanine.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:09.140
So this is your input
into your transcoder.

00:31:09.140 --> 00:31:12.290
That's comparing 1080
sources coming, comparing

00:31:12.290 --> 00:31:13.700
against the output,
which is what

00:31:13.700 --> 00:31:14.900
the customers are consuming.

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:19.160
So they might be gaining
1080 outputs, 720, 506,

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:20.633
so on and so forth.

00:31:20.633 --> 00:31:22.800
So this is really key-- and
I mentioned it earlier--

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:24.508
because you want to
make sure that you're

00:31:24.508 --> 00:31:27.680
putting good video out, that
you're not dropping frames,

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:32.060
that you're not having lip
sync issues, for example.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:33.650
So that's quite critical.

00:31:33.650 --> 00:31:35.608
And then you've got the
other green box, there,

00:31:35.608 --> 00:31:37.537
which is harmonic content prep.

00:31:37.537 --> 00:31:39.120
What I would call
out here is really--

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:40.953
do you see there's a
gray box in the middle?

00:31:40.953 --> 00:31:44.630
It says, availability zone 1.

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:45.860
And then it's surrounded by--

00:31:45.860 --> 00:31:48.360
it's a little bit hard to make
out on this big screen, here.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:49.568
But there's four boxes there.

00:31:49.568 --> 00:31:52.130
There's transcode,
package and storage.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.500
There's encryption
and origin server.

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:56.930
What you'll notice is,
there's a little Google

00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:59.030
Cloud in the middle there.

00:31:59.030 --> 00:32:00.560
As we've seen in
Melika's diagram,

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:02.518
before, Google Cloud is
a lot bigger than that.

00:32:02.518 --> 00:32:04.600
But I couldn't fit
it on the screen.

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:08.510
And then below, you've
got availability zone 2.

00:32:08.510 --> 00:32:10.670
Really key is that
we're doing this-- this

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:12.290
is highly available streaming.

00:32:12.290 --> 00:32:16.340
So in the normal world, we're
only just doing the top layer.

00:32:16.340 --> 00:32:19.565
And that's how we've been doing
it for the last 10 years or so.

00:32:19.565 --> 00:32:21.440
The innovation, really,
has come around being

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:22.700
able to do it in parallel.

00:32:22.700 --> 00:32:24.623
So you've got two parallel
streams coming in.

00:32:24.623 --> 00:32:26.040
So if you work
through the example

00:32:26.040 --> 00:32:29.805
from the left-hand side,
you have mezzanine output

00:32:29.805 --> 00:32:32.180
going over the Google Direct
Connect that I talked about.

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:34.680
That's 10 gigabits redundant,
coming from two different data

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:36.950
centers, coming
into a transcoder.

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:39.100
At the transcoder,
you've got this--

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:40.600
you see the blue
and the red arrows?

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:43.160
You're coming in and
there's a black arrow

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:44.690
in between the transcoders.

00:32:44.690 --> 00:32:45.978
This is synchronization.

00:32:45.978 --> 00:32:48.020
I show it in the yellow
box below, where it says,

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:50.280
frame accurate synchronization.

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:52.080
And frame accurate
is actually key,

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:54.380
because if you want to
put advertising on here,

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:57.230
you need to make sure
that it's frame accurate.

00:32:57.230 --> 00:32:59.860
Package and storage formats--
your HLS, your Smooth,

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:02.490
your DASH--

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:04.240
you need to also be
synchronized there.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:08.190
So you need to have a protocol
that basically says, I'm here.

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:08.700
I'm here.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:09.200
I'm here.

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:10.070
I'm here.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:13.170
Make sure that it's the
same, because otherwise,

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:14.960
on the right-hand
side where it says

00:33:14.960 --> 00:33:17.480
active active live
synchronized content, and then

00:33:17.480 --> 00:33:19.560
with the little
sort of globe there,

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:24.810
you can't do something funky
like go AZ1, AZ2, AZ1, AZ2.

00:33:24.810 --> 00:33:27.420
get Because you notice
those two red arrows allows

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:32.230
us to load balance live
content concurrently.

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:34.230
And actually, I think
Moore is going to give you

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:36.370
a little demo of that later.

00:33:36.370 --> 00:33:38.220
So here, we're
actually seeing that we

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:40.443
can use both legs in parallel.

00:33:40.443 --> 00:33:42.360
These are in two different
availability zones.

00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:44.578
So what it should say
there is that they're

00:33:44.578 --> 00:33:45.370
in the same region.

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:47.570
So if we deployed this--
let's use the US example.

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:48.480
We're west coast now.

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:51.120
So we went to Google's
region in US west coast.

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:52.450
And we deployed this here.

00:33:52.450 --> 00:33:55.560
We're putting it into
one AZ and a second AZ.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.440
So this means that if
the AZ had an issue, then

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:00.970
there is another one available
to provide customers.

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:04.560
So you've got redundancy
both at data-center level,

00:34:04.560 --> 00:34:07.080
within the availability zones,
but also at a channel level

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:10.620
because, going back
to my earlier example,

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:13.110
channel density was all
around, how many channels

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:14.949
can I fit into this server here?

00:34:14.949 --> 00:34:17.460
So if buy the most
expensive server,

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:21.070
usual economics works out that
I can get more channels on it.

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:23.070
Problem is that the fault
domain of that channel

00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.989
is the number of
channels you can get.

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:28.739
So if you can get five channels
on it and that box gets broken,

00:34:28.739 --> 00:34:30.420
then five channels are affected.

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:32.070
You have, now, an outage.

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:35.150
Go back to my example
about software upgrades.

00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:37.090
Software upgrades come
out every few months.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:37.940
They might fix bugs.

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:39.190
They might introduce features.

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:43.179
But when it happens, you're
going to take up five channels.

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:46.159
And then this
happens quite a lot.

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:49.850
So we're trying to avoid that.

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:51.380
So on my next slide--

00:34:53.967 --> 00:34:56.050
I've just talked previously
about happy path, when

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:57.850
everything's working great.

00:34:57.850 --> 00:34:59.950
So what happens when
something goes wrong?

00:34:59.950 --> 00:35:04.162
Because we can't assume
that everything is great.

00:35:04.162 --> 00:35:05.620
I've got something's
gone red here.

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:07.720
This is in availability zone 1.

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:10.420
My packager has gone wrong.

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:13.240
I don't really know-- perhaps
it's just this example--

00:35:13.240 --> 00:35:14.900
why that's happened.

00:35:14.900 --> 00:35:16.600
I don't really care, actually.

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:19.280
What I care about is
the customer experience.

00:35:19.280 --> 00:35:22.750
So the customers on the-- excuse
me-- on the right-hand side

00:35:22.750 --> 00:35:24.460
must carry on working.

00:35:24.460 --> 00:35:27.010
They must keep on
serving the CDNs.

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:28.930
They must keep filling
up the customer claims

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:32.407
buffer on the playback.

00:35:32.407 --> 00:35:33.490
And it must be consistent.

00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:36.500
So don't interrupt that
customer experience.

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.920
So the way that you go
about this, in this example,

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:41.570
is two things happen.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:44.390
Firstly is that you need
to detect there's a fault.

00:35:44.390 --> 00:35:50.840
So in the bottom box here, the
packager in AZ1 has failed.

00:35:50.840 --> 00:35:53.480
And so this means a
loss of resilience,

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:55.490
not a loss of channel.

00:35:55.490 --> 00:35:58.330
And that's a big distinction
because you're still on air.

00:35:58.330 --> 00:35:59.930
You're still making money.

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:02.390
And you're not getting
customer tickets.

00:36:02.390 --> 00:36:06.240
Customer's not going to call
the call center to say, hey,

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:08.663
I've just noticed you
had a loss of resilience,

00:36:08.663 --> 00:36:10.580
because they can still
watch "Game of Thrones"

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:13.130
or the football or something.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:16.697
So the second thing you need
to do, on the right-hand side,

00:36:16.697 --> 00:36:19.030
is, you see there's a red x
there where there previously

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:20.207
was an arrow.

00:36:20.207 --> 00:36:22.790
You need to be able to make sure
that you stop sending traffic

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:24.300
to the broken leg.

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:27.970
So in here, my example
in availability zone 1,

00:36:27.970 --> 00:36:29.170
that leg is gone.

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:32.430
Basically, it's not updating.

00:36:32.430 --> 00:36:33.920
So the manifest is not updating.

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:36.260
There's no fresh video
because the packager

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:37.730
is no longer outputting video.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:39.230
So therefore, it
can't be encrypted.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:41.780
Therefore, it can't be
delivered by Origim's server.

00:36:41.780 --> 00:36:44.080
And go back to my example.

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:47.500
AZ1 bad, AZ2 good.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:48.770
That model doesn't work.

00:36:48.770 --> 00:36:50.510
That's a bad
customer experience.

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:53.330
What you need to do is make sure
that you're only going to AZ2.

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:58.850
So it's AZ2, AZ2, AZ2, AZ2,
because the other leg is fine.

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:01.100
It's operationally good.

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:04.640
Now the way that we've
solved this problem

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.880
is, Kubernetes will do,
obviously, the monitoring.

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:11.540
It will detect and rebuild that
the packager in AZ1 has failed.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:13.370
I'll go and refer
you back to what

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:15.020
I was talking about
a few minutes ago

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:16.700
with the YAML files.

00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:21.330
The YAML files are the state
of the file of the channel.

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:23.930
So in the Sky
Sports 1 example, we

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:26.540
can return it back to how
it was 30 minutes ago,

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:27.890
before the fault occurred.

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:29.960
So we don't know
why it's happened.

00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:31.940
What we're trying to do
is restore that service

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.010
as quickly as we
can and put it back

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:37.050
to how it was 30 minutes ago.

00:37:37.050 --> 00:37:39.600
Once we've done that and
the health checks pass

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:42.420
and the channel
resilience is restored,

00:37:42.420 --> 00:37:45.990
we actually go back
to the previous slide,

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:48.130
where it's now in AZ1.

00:37:48.130 --> 00:37:50.300
It's actually working again.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:51.828
And that's, obviously,
really cool.

00:37:51.828 --> 00:37:54.120
I'll just call out the bottom
things on the encryption.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:55.872
There's a pay TV operator.

00:37:55.872 --> 00:37:57.330
We have digital
rights management--

00:37:57.330 --> 00:38:01.920
so the things like Play Ready,
Widevine, other DRMs, et

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:02.650
cetera.

00:38:02.650 --> 00:38:07.890
And Origin, obviously, is
storing out and encrypted.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.930
The tools that we
use to do this,

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:11.820
to actually recover service--

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.180
obviously, we operate our
own Kubernetes cluster.

00:38:14.180 --> 00:38:17.680
In the two AZs, we have
independent clusters.

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:20.400
So the one in AZ1 is
independent of AZ2.

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:22.170
So we run them in parallel.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:25.888
And we're doing the same
workload in parallel, twice.

00:38:25.888 --> 00:38:26.680
We're using Source.

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:28.170
Source is something
that we wrote

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.780
ourself, which is our
configuration management tool.

00:38:30.780 --> 00:38:32.490
And it basically goes
and manages that.

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:36.720
So we use, obviously,
things like Git repos.

00:38:36.720 --> 00:38:38.800
And when an engineer
wants to make changes--

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:41.383
for example, they have to check
in and they have to check out.

00:38:41.383 --> 00:38:43.440
We've got version
control on it, as well.

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:48.310
And I don't have a slide on
it, but from a confidence point

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:52.405
of view, we actually go
through five different realms

00:38:52.405 --> 00:38:54.100
on all our paths to production.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:55.758
We go through a
development cycle.

00:38:55.758 --> 00:38:57.300
So we have a
development environment.

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:58.800
We go through a
test environment.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:00.800
We go through a non-functional
test environment,

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:02.300
where we're injecting load.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:04.660
And we go through a
stage, which is all

00:39:04.660 --> 00:39:07.480
about testing and making
sure that it's reliable,

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:09.310
because it's very
important in video.

00:39:09.310 --> 00:39:11.940
It's no good if it stays up
for 24 hours and falls over.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:14.080
You need it to
run for two weeks.

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:15.760
And then, obviously,
in production--

00:39:15.760 --> 00:39:17.676
show what you're doing.

00:39:17.676 --> 00:39:21.130
The one of the things
that I will just finish on

00:39:21.130 --> 00:39:24.310
is, when we talk to
Sky Sports about this,

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:27.370
we went through the
economics of the cloud.

00:39:27.370 --> 00:39:29.440
One of the things
that was called out

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:31.340
was, we did the number
crunching on it,

00:39:31.340 --> 00:39:32.950
and they had a system
that they've had

00:39:32.950 --> 00:39:34.790
operational for eight years.

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:37.090
And when we went
through and said, look,

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:38.838
that hardware is no
longer supported.

00:39:38.838 --> 00:39:39.880
We can't get spare parts.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:42.050
We can't get software
upgrades for it.

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:43.570
We need to go and replace that.

00:39:43.570 --> 00:39:45.850
So we came out, and
it was a big number.

00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:48.420
It was a high six-figure number.

00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:50.370
When we went through
them and said, look,

00:39:50.370 --> 00:39:52.120
how often do you
actually use that system?

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:56.410
Because the Formula One's
only on once every other week.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:39:58.670
The football's on
several times a week.

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:01.300
What do you do at this time of
day when there's no football on

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:02.870
and there's no Formula One?

00:40:02.870 --> 00:40:04.580
Well, it's just sat there.

00:40:04.580 --> 00:40:07.480
Well, maybe that's not the
cost-effective way to do it.

00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:10.023
So we asked them, how many hours
of programming do you have?

00:40:10.023 --> 00:40:12.190
It turned out to be about
1,000 hours of programming

00:40:12.190 --> 00:40:14.740
per year for various events.

00:40:14.740 --> 00:40:16.363
And we said, well,
surely there's

00:40:16.363 --> 00:40:18.280
got to be more cost-effective
way of doing it.

00:40:18.280 --> 00:40:20.640
Could we consider
an opex-type thing

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:22.910
and move those
workloads to the cloud?

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:23.800
And so we did that.

00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:26.535
And it is significant savings.

00:40:26.535 --> 00:40:29.380
It's less than half the cost.

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:30.550
And it's more flexible.

00:40:30.550 --> 00:40:32.320
And we don't have
all those pain points

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:33.700
that we talked about before.

00:40:33.700 --> 00:40:35.930
We don't have to worry
about infrastructure.

00:40:35.930 --> 00:40:38.870
That's Google's problem.

00:40:38.870 --> 00:40:42.070
We don't have to worry
about storage, for example.

00:40:42.070 --> 00:40:43.570
That's Google's problem.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:46.090
Load balancing-- it's
Google's problem.

00:40:46.090 --> 00:40:49.670
Part of the-- there we go.

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:50.170
Thank you.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:51.787
I'm overtime.

00:40:51.787 --> 00:40:53.120
MOORE MACAULEY: Hello, everyone.

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:53.830
Thank you, Jeff.

00:40:53.830 --> 00:40:56.050
Thank you for a very
interesting presentation.

00:40:56.050 --> 00:40:58.990
You get to enjoy another accent.

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:00.600
I'm originally from Ireland.

00:41:00.600 --> 00:41:03.467
And I'm going to try and speak
very slowly, because I've

00:41:03.467 --> 00:41:05.800
got a tendency, when I'm
nervous in front of big crowds,

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:08.120
to speak awfully quickly.

00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:10.160
Thank you very much.

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:12.970
So I'm from Harmonic.

00:41:12.970 --> 00:41:15.610
And we believe in smarter,
faster, simpler video

00:41:15.610 --> 00:41:16.660
streaming.

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:20.260
And we were very excited to be
involved with Sky and Google

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:22.860
on this project.

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:26.340
Just a little bit of
background on who Harmonic are.

00:41:26.340 --> 00:41:28.230
Some of you may never
have heard of us,

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:29.880
although I'm sure
that over the years,

00:41:29.880 --> 00:41:31.680
you've watched
video that's flowed

00:41:31.680 --> 00:41:33.840
through many of our devices.

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:36.070
We've been around
for over 20 years.

00:41:36.070 --> 00:41:39.930
We are headquartered
in Silicon Valley.

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:41.950
If you happen to
drive along the 237,

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:44.110
we're about North First Street.

00:41:44.110 --> 00:41:49.200
Around the world, we have over
5,000 customers in the media

00:41:49.200 --> 00:41:54.500
and service provider sector.

00:41:54.500 --> 00:41:57.470
We started off
building appliances.

00:41:57.470 --> 00:42:00.920
And if we look at the number
of over-the-top channels

00:42:00.920 --> 00:42:03.705
we have deployed, we
entered the streaming market

00:42:03.705 --> 00:42:06.420
about when Jeff
did, many years ago.

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:10.220
We have 38,000 live
channels deployed today.

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:13.580
Most of those channels are
deployed on appliances.

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:16.670
But many of them are now
deployed in our software,

00:42:16.670 --> 00:42:18.320
as a service offering.

00:42:18.320 --> 00:42:21.710
In fact, if I look at our
software as a service offering,

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:25.400
for our customers, we
actually deliver video

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:29.840
from our origin servers to over
6.5 million subscribers who

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:33.050
pay those customers
of ours every month

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:36.810
for the video services
that they watch.

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:39.210
Subscription video
is really difficult.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:41.610
As Jeff has described, you
have to provide a really

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:43.550
high quality of service.

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.780
And our goal these days
is really to make sure

00:42:45.780 --> 00:42:48.570
that we maximize that
quality of experience

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:51.300
for our customer's customers.

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:52.470
So how did we do that?

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:55.070
How did we make the transition
from what was effectively

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:58.130
a box manufacturer in
Silicon Valley to someone

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:02.080
who provides a software
as a service offering?

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:05.960
It was no small change.

00:43:05.960 --> 00:43:09.070
But as the product
architect, it was my job,

00:43:09.070 --> 00:43:12.430
six years ago, to kick
off a project that

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:15.850
looked at everything Harmonic
did for its customers

00:43:15.850 --> 00:43:18.550
from end to end and
to think about how

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:23.020
we could re-envisage that
from a hardware delivery

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:25.180
to a software delivery.

00:43:25.180 --> 00:43:27.100
For customers, we take
care of many things.

00:43:27.100 --> 00:43:29.830
We take care of
ingesting content.

00:43:29.830 --> 00:43:34.750
We take care of play out,
graphics, transcoding,

00:43:34.750 --> 00:43:35.950
over the top.

00:43:35.950 --> 00:43:39.770
Even into the traditional cable
world and satellite world,

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:42.400
we provide statistical
multiplexing solutions.

00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:43.780
We encrypt the content.

00:43:43.780 --> 00:43:44.860
We package it.

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:47.590
We provide origin serving
functionality, manifest

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:49.530
manipulation, and monitoring.

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:51.520
And there was a
very traditional way

00:43:51.520 --> 00:43:53.920
of delivering that in
the appliance world--

00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:57.910
very simple in terms of boxes
delivering functionality.

00:43:57.910 --> 00:44:02.050
Everybody understood
exactly how it was deployed.

00:44:02.050 --> 00:44:04.330
What we needed to do was,
we needed to think about it,

00:44:04.330 --> 00:44:05.590
reinvent it.

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:08.530
Reinvent it and
deliver something that

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:10.780
has a different architecture.

00:44:10.780 --> 00:44:12.820
It's based on containers.

00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:14.670
And think about all
of those problems

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:15.670
that you have described.

00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:19.000
How do you effectively provide
one-for-one resilience?

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:21.490
How do you provide
upgradability?

00:44:21.490 --> 00:44:25.420
And we did that in
a product that we

00:44:25.420 --> 00:44:28.960
called VOS, which is our
cloud-native media processing

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:29.820
platform.

00:44:29.820 --> 00:44:32.020
And it's what Jeff used.

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:35.410
And we also deliver that
as a SAS offering, vOS 360,

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:38.750
to our customers.

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:41.210
When I look at Sky,
Sky gave me all

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:43.280
of the usual requirements
that anyone starts off

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:44.990
with a live project.

00:44:44.990 --> 00:44:47.650
They really wanted to hit all
of the devices they needed

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:49.520
to use, all of the
streaming formats,

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:51.620
to make sure they got
into those devices.

00:44:51.620 --> 00:44:52.880
Closed captioning.

00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:55.430
They need timed metadata
because they were doing sports.

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:57.638
You want to be able to know
that a goal has gone off,

00:44:57.638 --> 00:44:59.210
send notifications.

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:01.970
And they really wanted
ease of installation,

00:45:01.970 --> 00:45:05.150
ease of monitoring,
and ease of recovery.

00:45:05.150 --> 00:45:07.370
This is what I hear
from every customer.

00:45:07.370 --> 00:45:10.040
But what was different
about Sky's requirements

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:13.160
was that desire to
make it better, that

00:45:13.160 --> 00:45:16.290
no single point of failure.

00:45:16.290 --> 00:45:18.110
And although Jeff
didn't mention it,

00:45:18.110 --> 00:45:21.290
he also put a second
constraint on it, which was he

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:23.842
didn't want any additional
delay to his sports.

00:45:23.842 --> 00:45:24.800
Because, you know what?

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:28.880
We could have built a very,
very redundant system, but if we

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.350
had delayed another 30 seconds.

00:45:30.350 --> 00:45:31.670
But he said no.

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:33.710
Don't want any more
delay to my sports.

00:45:33.710 --> 00:45:36.050
They need to be
faster, not slower.

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:38.080
So that made it a
little bit harder.

00:45:41.470 --> 00:45:44.750
I think we've been 45 minutes
into this presentation.

00:45:44.750 --> 00:45:46.170
And it's time you
saw some video.

00:45:46.170 --> 00:45:48.990
But before I do, I'm just
going to show you one diagram.

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:51.180
This is the diagram
of the video that I'm

00:45:51.180 --> 00:45:53.160
going to show you, how
we put it together.

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:55.050
We deployed it on Google Cloud.

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:57.180
We went a little bit
farther than Jeff asked for.

00:45:57.180 --> 00:46:00.720
We put it in separate
zones, not just

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.600
separate availability zones.

00:46:03.600 --> 00:46:10.230
And we used GKE, not GCP,
because I like it when Google

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:11.640
deploys Kubernetes for me.

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:14.015
Like Jeff said, I don't want
to worry about that, either.

00:46:16.080 --> 00:46:17.850
We built this system.

00:46:17.850 --> 00:46:19.950
Let's see what
that A/B switching

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:23.490
looks like from a
customer standpoint.

00:46:23.490 --> 00:46:24.870
So here's some
beautiful content.

00:46:24.870 --> 00:46:26.680
And while you watch
the beautiful content,

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:28.620
it does say it's in 4K and HDR.

00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:30.583
And although I'm standing
in a theater today,

00:46:30.583 --> 00:46:32.250
I'm really sorry to
tell you what you're

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:34.740
watching isn't in 4K and HDR.

00:46:34.740 --> 00:46:37.347
I wish that it was.

00:46:37.347 --> 00:46:39.180
However, if at home you
would love to see it

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:41.280
and you happen to have
a Roku streaming device,

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:44.700
there is a NASA channel on
the Roku streaming device

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:46.500
that we provide the
back end for that

00:46:46.500 --> 00:46:49.650
will allow you to see this
same content in 4K and HDR.

00:46:49.650 --> 00:46:51.900
And breathtaking as
the content is here,

00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:54.278
it's even more
breathtaking there.

00:46:54.278 --> 00:46:55.820
While you've been
watching the video,

00:46:55.820 --> 00:46:58.610
perhaps you've
noticed the VOS 360

00:46:58.610 --> 00:47:00.890
logo bopping back and
forward from the left

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:02.090
and right to the screen.

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:04.040
That wasn't put there
by a professional.

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:06.440
That was put there
by me, which is why

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:08.880
it's so accurately positioned.

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:11.490
So what I did,
actually, was I went

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:13.680
into those two
transcoding instances

00:47:13.680 --> 00:47:15.250
that Jeff showed you previously.

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:18.330
And I configured
one of them to put

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.280
the logo on the left
side and the other one

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:21.990
to put the logo
on the right side.

00:47:21.990 --> 00:47:24.780
A little bit of a difference.

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:27.330
And so that's why you get
to see it popping back

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:29.190
and forward from either side.

00:47:29.190 --> 00:47:30.690
But it is beautiful
content, and I

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:32.565
do encourage you to go
and have a look at it.

00:47:32.565 --> 00:47:36.270
I'm a great believer in NASA.

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:36.970
I love it.

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:38.760
And I love the
content they produce.

00:47:38.760 --> 00:47:42.630
Some of the moon rises
are amazing to watch.

00:47:42.630 --> 00:47:45.690
We did go even further than
just zone 1 and zone 2.

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:48.250
We offer our software
not just as a SAS.

00:47:48.250 --> 00:47:50.580
I told you, we sold it
to customers on premise.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:53.490
So here is another diagram
where you could actually

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:57.780
do that same synchronization
with an on-premise cluster

00:47:57.780 --> 00:48:02.560
and a cluster in GCP or GKE.

00:48:02.560 --> 00:48:05.710
I think we actually did
deliver on what Sky wanted.

00:48:05.710 --> 00:48:07.600
And the challenge that
we really had to solve

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:10.060
was this challenge
called the CAP Theorem,

00:48:10.060 --> 00:48:12.700
or Brewer's Theorem, for
the computer scientists.

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:14.680
Among you, we already
know what it is.

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:17.950
For those of us who don't
understand, basically,

00:48:17.950 --> 00:48:20.590
if we partition two things
and we drop information

00:48:20.590 --> 00:48:23.020
between them, then we have
to make a decision about

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:26.800
whether or not we
serve every request.

00:48:26.800 --> 00:48:28.990
And sometimes, the
requests are different.

00:48:28.990 --> 00:48:32.200
Or we only serve
requests from one side

00:48:32.200 --> 00:48:34.788
when we know there are problems.

00:48:34.788 --> 00:48:36.830
We don't want the video
to jump back and forward.

00:48:36.830 --> 00:48:38.663
It's got to look seamless,
like it did here.

00:48:38.663 --> 00:48:41.370
So we sacrifice in availability.

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:43.998
And the whole challenge was to
make your availability numbers

00:48:43.998 --> 00:48:45.790
for that backup system
as high as possible.

00:48:48.530 --> 00:48:49.950
That delivered Sky benefits.

00:48:49.950 --> 00:48:52.130
It gave them the
improved false tolerance.

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:55.070
It gave them the ability
to do green-blue upgrades.

00:48:55.070 --> 00:48:58.520
It gave them the ability
to do A/B testing.

00:48:58.520 --> 00:49:01.180
Way more than we
originally thought.

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:02.930
Often, we started off
looking at the fault

00:49:02.930 --> 00:49:05.980
tolerance of this project.

00:49:05.980 --> 00:49:08.530
I think, as Harmonic
in this project,

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:11.450
we really did deliver
smarter, faster,

00:49:11.450 --> 00:49:13.840
simpler video streaming.

00:49:13.840 --> 00:49:18.140
And it's working with
customers like Sky

00:49:18.140 --> 00:49:19.900
and working with
people like Google that

00:49:19.900 --> 00:49:22.270
enables us to deliver
on some of the most

00:49:22.270 --> 00:49:24.790
fantastic projects in the
world with some of the greatest

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:25.720
content--

00:49:25.720 --> 00:49:27.010
Formula One, NASA.

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:29.440
It's a great place to be.

00:49:29.440 --> 00:49:32.230
Melika, do you have a
question for both of us?

00:49:32.230 --> 00:49:33.550
Or are we out of time?

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:34.710
MELIKA GOLKARAM: I
think we're out of time.

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:35.080
MOORE MACAULEY: All right.

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:36.705
Well, I don't get to
answer a question.

00:49:36.705 --> 00:49:38.080
Thank you very much, indeed.

00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:38.830
Thank you, Melika.

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:40.030
Thank you, Jeff.

00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:41.670
It's been great to be here.

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:42.790
I hope you enjoyed it.

00:49:42.790 --> 00:49:46.140
[APPLAUSE]

